{
  "id": "p16",
  "title": "Pointers",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "p16-l1",
      "title": "^ and new",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A pointer holds the address of a value instead of the value itself. var p: ^integer; declares a pointer to an integer.",
        "new(p) asks for fresh memory while the program runs and makes p point to it. p^ (say \"p hat\") is the value p points to: p^ := 42;",
        "Two pointers can point to the same memory. Then a change through one is seen through the other."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Ptr;",
          "var",
          "  p, q: ^integer;",
          "begin",
          "  new(p);",
          "  p^ := 42;",
          "  writeln(p^);",
          "  q := p;",
          "  q^ := 7;",
          "  writeln(p^);",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          "42",
          "7"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p16-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does a pointer hold?",
          "options": [
            "The address of a value",
            "A copy of a value",
            "Only integers",
            "A file name"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "It points to where the value is."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does new(p) do?",
          "options": [
            "Gets fresh memory and makes p point to it",
            "Sets p to 0",
            "Deletes p",
            "Copies p"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Memory while the program runs, from the heap."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l1-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Store 10 where p points.",
          "code": [
            "  p___ := 10;"
          ],
          "options": [
            "^",
            "",
            "@",
            "*"
          ],
          "answer": "^",
          "explanation": "p^ is the value p points to."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "program Same;",
            "var",
            "  p, q: ^integer;",
            "begin",
            "  new(p);",
            "  p^ := 5;",
            "  q := p;",
            "  q^ := 99;",
            "  writeln(p^);",
            "end."
          ],
          "options": [
            "99",
            "5",
            "0",
            "an error"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "q points to the same memory as p."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l1-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program P;",
            "var",
            "  p: ^integer;",
            "begin",
            "  new(p);",
            "  p := 5;",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 5,
          "explanation": "p is a pointer. To store 5 where it points, write p^ := 5;"
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l1-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How do you say p^?",
          "options": [
            "\"p hat\" or \"what p points to\"",
            "\"p power\"",
            "\"p up\"",
            "\"p xor\""
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The hat follows the pointer."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p16-l2",
      "title": "nil and dispose",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "nil is a pointer that points nowhere. Following it with p^ crashes: Runtime error 216. So check if p <> nil first.",
        "dispose(p) gives the memory back. After that, p^ is off limits: using it is a bug (Runtime error 204: Invalid pointer operation).",
        "Memory you never give back is a memory leak. On a PC with 640 KB, that hurt quickly."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program NilDemo;",
          "var",
          "  p: ^integer;",
          "begin",
          "  p := nil;",
          "  if p = nil then",
          "    writeln('p points nowhere');",
          "  new(p);",
          "  p^ := 3;",
          "  writeln('p^ = ', p^);",
          "  dispose(p);",
          "  p := nil;",
          "  writeln('Memory given back');",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          "p points nowhere",
          "p^ = 3",
          "Memory given back"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p16-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is nil?",
          "options": [
            "A pointer that points nowhere",
            "The number 0",
            "An empty string",
            "A deleted variable"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Following nil crashes the program."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does dispose(p) do?",
          "options": [
            "Gives p's memory back",
            "Sets p^ to 0",
            "Makes a new pointer",
            "Ends the program"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The counterpart of new."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l2-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Only follow p if it points somewhere.",
          "code": [
            "  if p <> ___ then",
            "    writeln(p^);"
          ],
          "options": [
            "nil",
            "0",
            "null",
            "''"
          ],
          "answer": "nil",
          "explanation": "Pascal calls the empty pointer nil."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l2-e4",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program crashes. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program Crash;",
            "var",
            "  p: ^integer;",
            "begin",
            "  new(p);",
            "  p^ := 1;",
            "  dispose(p);",
            "  writeln(p^);",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 7,
          "explanation": "After dispose(p), the memory isn't yours anymore."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l2-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does Runtime error 216 mean here?",
          "options": [
            "A nil pointer was followed",
            "Division by zero",
            "The disk is full",
            "A file is missing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "General protection fault: the address was nowhere."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is a memory leak?",
          "options": [
            "Memory from new that's never given back",
            "A pointer to nil",
            "A broken disk",
            "A wrong index"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The program uses more and more memory."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p16-l3",
      "title": "Pointers to Records",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Pointers really shine with records. A record can contain a pointer to its own kind. Because the record isn't declared yet, the pointer type comes first: type PNode = ^TNode; TNode = record … next: PNode; end;",
        "p^.value is the field value of the record p points to.",
        "These records linked by pointers are called nodes. They are the building blocks of lists, trees and more."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Nodes;",
          "type",
          "  PNode = ^TNode;",
          "  TNode = record",
          "    value: integer;",
          "    next: PNode;",
          "  end;",
          "var",
          "  a, b: PNode;",
          "begin",
          "  new(a);",
          "  new(b);",
          "  a^.value := 1;",
          "  b^.value := 2;",
          "  a^.next := b;",
          "  b^.next := nil;",
          "  writeln(a^.value, ' -> ', a^.next^.value);",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          "1 -> 2"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p16-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why can PNode = ^TNode come before TNode is declared?",
          "options": [
            "Pascal allows pointer types to name a record declared right after",
            "It can't",
            "Because of new",
            "Because TNode is global"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "This is the one place where Pascal lets you use a name before it's declared."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is p^.value?",
          "options": [
            "The value field of the record p points to",
            "The address of value",
            "p's own value",
            "An error"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Follow the pointer, then take the field."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l3-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Link node a to node b.",
          "code": [
            "  a^___next := b;"
          ],
          "options": [
            ".",
            "^",
            "->",
            ","
          ],
          "answer": ".",
          "explanation": "a^ is the record, .next its field."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l3-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "program Link;",
            "type",
            "  PNode = ^TNode;",
            "  TNode = record",
            "    value: integer;",
            "    next: PNode;",
            "  end;",
            "var",
            "  x, y: PNode;",
            "begin",
            "  new(x);",
            "  new(y);",
            "  x^.value := 10;",
            "  y^.value := 20;",
            "  x^.next := y;",
            "  writeln(x^.next^.value);",
            "end."
          ],
          "options": [
            "20",
            "10",
            "an error",
            "nil"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "x^.next is y, and y^.value is 20."
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l3-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program N;",
            "type",
            "  PNode = ^TNode;",
            "  TNode = record",
            "    value: integer;",
            "  end;",
            "var",
            "  p: PNode;",
            "begin",
            "  new(p);",
            "  p.value := 5;",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 10,
          "explanation": "p is a pointer: write p^.value := 5;"
        },
        {
          "id": "p16-l3-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What do you call records linked by pointers?",
          "options": [
            "Nodes",
            "Files",
            "Sets",
            "Arrays"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Lists, trees and graphs are made of nodes."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
